  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Monday)\nIssued at 1159 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nBroad surface high pressure persists across the eastern CONUS\nwith a couple of surface lows over the Great Basin and Desert\nSouthwest. Aloft, a modified Omega Block resides across the CONUS\nwith broad troughing across the Great Basin, ridging across the\nPlains and Mississippi Valley, and troughing extending from the\nGulf into the Appalachians.\n\nDry conditions and a warming trend will continue through late\nweek as the subtropical ridge builds poleward into the\nMississippi and Ohio River Valleys. A highly anomalous 582 dam\nridge will build across the Lower Mississippi Valley through\nFriday, resulting in strong subsidence and warming temperatures.\nHighs will run about 10 to 15 degrees above normal for mid-\nNovember, reaching the upper 60s to lower 70s.\n\nMedium-range guidance remains consistent with the ridge breaking\ndown by late week, establishing broad southwesterly flow across\nthe eastern CONUS. A shortwave will eject out of the\nIntermountain West this weekend, pushing a cold front through the\nMid-South on Sunday. With limited moisture return ahead of FROPA,\nlight rainfall chances remain low (<15%). Near-normal\ntemperatures will return behind the front and persist into\nThanksgiving week.",
